WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:07.459
Hey everyone! In this video we'll take a look at some of the limitations of translucent materials

2
00:00:07.459 --> 00:00:12.580
using Lumen and how we can avoid them to recreate a realistic look that we want.

3
00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:17.859
We'll also be exploring some advanced techniques for edge control in the engine.

4
00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.219
Let's get started!

5
00:00:19.859 --> 00:00:24.260
Alright, as I just mentioned, we'll be reviewing how certain looks and feels can still be

6
00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:29.940
replicated in engine without the use of the Fresnel node, using something like the dot product,

7
00:00:30.019 --> 00:00:32.500
the power, and the transform vector node.

8
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:37.859
And knowing alternatives to the Fresnel index workflow that we just learned in the previous

9
00:00:37.859 --> 00:00:44.100
class is really important because view-dependent effects like edge highlighting and transparency

10
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.420
calculations may not really receive proper dynamic lighting using Lumen.

11
00:00:48.979 --> 00:00:55.299
We'll also get an overview on faking caustics and hot spots in Unreal without the use of

12
00:00:55.299 --> 00:00:56.979
something called Path Tracer.

13
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.960
Because translucent reflections and materials aren't really properly captured in Lumen,

14
00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.519
so for example they don't really contribute to light bounces onto other surfaces

15
00:01:07.519 --> 00:01:09.760
and can really cast proper shadows.

16
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.200
So let's jump into the engine and see how we can still achieve what we want.

17
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.060
Okay, we're in the faking details and refraction area of our default map.

18
00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:23.180
And if I navigate here and focus on the floor,

19
00:01:23.739 --> 00:01:26.859
you can see this faking refraction kind of effect.

20
00:01:28.099 --> 00:01:31.459
Now, it's important to acknowledge that without Path Tracer,

21
00:01:31.459 --> 00:01:35.540
you cannot really get bounce lights like this on translucent materials.

22
00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.620
But we can fake this using something called Decal Components,

23
00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:44.900
with a specific texture assigned to them replicating this caustics or refracted look.

24
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:50.180
And if I press G and select my Decal Component, you can see what I mean.

25
00:01:52.319 --> 00:01:58.239
Now, Decal Components are really just planes with translucent or additive blend modes

26
00:01:58.239 --> 00:02:01.040
that wrap around surfaces like this

27
00:02:01.599 --> 00:02:06.000
and are typically used to add details to our environments.

28
00:02:07.019 --> 00:02:09.740
Now, let's select our Decal Component over here

29
00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.620
and double click on its material to see how it was made.

30
00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:18.460
Okay, so it might look a little bit messy, but this material is actually pretty simple.

31
00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:22.300
First of all, let's acknowledge if we click in an empty area of the graph

32
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:26.939
that the material domain was changed from surface to deferred decal,

33
00:02:26.939 --> 00:02:29.419
and the blend mode assigned is the translucent.

34
00:02:29.979 --> 00:02:32.780
So in this material, we're really just taking a simple texture

35
00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.020
that I'm gonna be opening up down here

36
00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:40.159
that can be easily hand painted in any image editing software.

37
00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:44.080
And then if I switch the preview mode to plane,

38
00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.319
you can see how if applied to plane and then onto a surface,

39
00:02:48.319 --> 00:02:51.759
it can easily recreate this refracted look.

40
00:02:51.759 --> 00:02:54.000
Now, we have a linear gradient node here

41
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.680
that is attached to an add node that is not connected to anything.

42
00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.919
We left it here just for you to play around

43
00:02:59.919 --> 00:03:02.800
and you can connect it to any of the material properties.

44
00:03:03.919 --> 00:03:07.360
We're done multiplying the base color with a really high value

45
00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.919
to increase the emission of our decal.

46
00:03:09.919 --> 00:03:12.719
And we have various values here that you can really play around with.

47
00:03:13.439 --> 00:03:15.599
Now, keep in mind that decals materials

48
00:03:15.599 --> 00:03:18.879
are much more limited than standard materials

49
00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:21.919
because they're projected onto existing surfaces

50
00:03:21.919 --> 00:03:24.639
rather than being full materials themselves.

51
00:03:24.639 --> 00:03:27.840
So they don't really have any physical properties

52
00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.000
and have very limited blend modes.

53
00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.759
So you won't be able to push this in all the directions you want,

54
00:03:33.759 --> 00:03:37.199
but everything in here is at your disposal to test things out.

55
00:03:37.919 --> 00:03:39.759
All right, let's go back to our viewport.

56
00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.379
And if we navigate more here on the right,

57
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:49.139
we can see some examples of using the power and dot node

58
00:03:49.139 --> 00:03:51.379
to replicate the Fresnel effect.

59
00:03:51.379 --> 00:03:54.020
Now, these two materials are not translucent

60
00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:56.979
and they don't have any altered refraction index.

61
00:03:57.539 --> 00:04:00.419
They're also not using any Fresnel nodes.

62
00:04:00.419 --> 00:04:03.539
And yet they do replicate how the light would bounce

63
00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:07.780
on surfaces such as silk in the first example, and cotton.

64
00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.930
So let's select the first preview mesh

65
00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.969
and then open up its material from the Details panel

66
00:04:13.969 --> 00:04:15.889
to see how these materials were developed.

67
00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:19.949
Okay, so this material is a bit more complex

68
00:04:19.949 --> 00:04:22.189
than the simple decal we just examined.

69
00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:24.990
And we'll just go over it together

70
00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:27.149
because we're introducing some new nodes here.

71
00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.939
So we're starting with a camera vector

72
00:04:29.939 --> 00:04:33.060
that I'm just going to be plugging directly into the base color

73
00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.180
to show you what it does.

74
00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.300
I'll just be expanding this preview a little bit.

75
00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.339
Okay, so the camera vector tells us the direction

76
00:04:40.339 --> 00:04:44.259
of the camera from any point on our material surface.

77
00:04:44.259 --> 00:04:46.980
And that's what you see here in this color information.

78
00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:50.259
The camera vector is super useful for creating effects

79
00:04:50.259 --> 00:04:52.420
that change based on the viewing angle

80
00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.379
like the Fresnel effect that we're trying to recreate here.

81
00:04:56.019 --> 00:04:58.740
Now, it's important to know that the camera vector

82
00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.939
outputs a direction vector in world space.

83
00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:05.860
And we're using this dot product to compare it

84
00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:07.699
with another kind of information.

85
00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.480
And if we follow the noodle down here,

86
00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.079
you can see that this dot product

87
00:05:12.079 --> 00:05:14.720
is connected to a transform vector,

88
00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:17.120
which is connected to a texture sample

89
00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.439
that has a black normal texture.

90
00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.879
If I connect the RGB output of our normal

91
00:05:22.879 --> 00:05:23.759
to the base color,

92
00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.490
you can see that the color information displayed

93
00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:30.449
is completely different from the camera vector.

94
00:05:30.449 --> 00:05:33.730
And that's because normal maps are usually stored

95
00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:35.410
in tangent space,

96
00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:37.970
while camera vectors, as I just mentioned,

97
00:05:38.769 --> 00:05:40.769
speak in world space.

98
00:05:41.569 --> 00:05:44.050
So these two different color informations

99
00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:46.290
are basically asking two different questions.

100
00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:50.379
The tangent space normal map is asking,

101
00:05:50.379 --> 00:05:54.300
what is the app vector for a specific point on the surface?

102
00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.500
While the world space direction vector

103
00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:59.579
of the camera vector is asking,

104
00:05:59.579 --> 00:06:02.379
what is the app vector for the entire world?

105
00:06:03.019 --> 00:06:05.420
So basically, the normal map is speaking

106
00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:07.259
from an object point of view,

107
00:06:07.259 --> 00:06:08.620
is object oriented,

108
00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:11.819
while the camera vector is world oriented.

109
00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:15.660
And that's where this transform vector node

110
00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:16.939
comes into place.

111
00:06:16.939 --> 00:06:18.860
Because if we click on this arrow here,

112
00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:20.220
we can expand it,

113
00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.259
and we can see that it's basically translating

114
00:06:23.259 --> 00:06:25.420
the tangent space color information

115
00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:27.259
into the world space,

116
00:06:27.259 --> 00:06:30.139
so that these two nodes can speak the same language.

117
00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:33.420
So if I connect the transform vector to the base color,

118
00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:35.980
we can see here that the color information

119
00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:38.779
is much more similar to the camera vector one.

120
00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:42.029
Now, if we connect the dot product

121
00:06:42.029 --> 00:06:45.709
to the output base color of our material,

122
00:06:46.579 --> 00:06:48.420
we can see here what's going on.

123
00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:50.100
So connected to the camera vector

124
00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:51.620
and the transform vector,

125
00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:53.620
the dot product is asking us,

126
00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:56.180
how directly am I looking at this surface?

127
00:06:57.089 --> 00:06:58.930
Because if I'm looking straight at it,

128
00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:01.889
I will be resulting a value of one,

129
00:07:02.529 --> 00:07:04.850
while if I'm looking at it from the side,

130
00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.689
I will be resulting a value of zero.

131
00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:10.449
This resulting into a black edge outline

132
00:07:11.329 --> 00:07:13.170
and an inner white color.

133
00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:16.769
Now, to recreate something like the cotton,

134
00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:19.170
we want to invert this result

135
00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:20.850
by using a one minus node,

136
00:07:21.569 --> 00:07:24.850
and make the edge much more defined and fuzzy.

137
00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:28.290
So if I connected this add node to the base color,

138
00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:29.490
you can see what I mean.

139
00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:32.779
Now, the cotton and the silk

140
00:07:32.779 --> 00:07:35.740
are almost opposite in what they want to achieve.

141
00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:38.699
What I mean by that is that, as you can see,

142
00:07:38.699 --> 00:07:40.860
the one minus node is used for the cotton,

143
00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:42.779
but it's not used with the silk.

144
00:07:42.779 --> 00:07:45.420
And we can actually play around with these values

145
00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:48.220
and invert them to have a really cool effect.

146
00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.860
So if I switch these 20s to zeros,

147
00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:54.939
and these zeros to 20s,

148
00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:58.829
we have something like an inverted eye

149
00:07:58.829 --> 00:08:00.509
that's constantly staring at us.

150
00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:02.589
Let's make it a real eye.

151
00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:05.550
So if after the add node,

152
00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:07.230
I add a one minus node,

153
00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:10.829
we'll basically invert the entire polar result.

154
00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:14.110
And here we have a pupil constantly staring at us,

155
00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:15.149
which I think is pretty cool,

156
00:08:15.149 --> 00:08:16.509
and I wanted to show you that.

157
00:08:16.509 --> 00:08:17.870
All right, that was it for this video.

158
00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:19.149
I hope you had a good time.

159
00:08:19.149 --> 00:08:20.509
In the next video,

160
00:08:20.509 --> 00:08:23.470
we'll learn how to develop a bump offset effect

161
00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:25.310
on a translucent material

162
00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:28.829
and dive deeper into the bump offset workflows and techniques.

163
00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:30.269
I'll see you there.
